    {
      "id": "CO-ING-8-2026-W18-gerunds-infinitives-001-MASTERY-v8",
      "statement": "\"I like **dancing**\" vs. \"I'd like **to dance**.\"\nWhat is the difference?",
      "context": "Understanding preference nuance.",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "They mean exactly the same.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "feedback": "Incorrect."
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "One is a general habit, the other is a specific desire for now.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "feedback": "Correct! \"Like + ing\" is for general tastes; \"Would like + to\" is a polite request/desire."
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Both are about the past.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "feedback": "Incorrect."
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "The second one is rude.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "feedback": "Incorrect."
        }
      ],
      "correct_answer": "B",
      "explanation": "\"I like + -ing\" describe un gusto general. \"I would like (I'd like) + to\" se usa para pedir algo o expresar un deseo específico en un momento dado.\n\n---",
      "difficulty": "8",
      "images": [],
      "tags": [],
      "bloom": "Analyze",
      "icfes_type": "Pragmática",
      "expected_success": 0.5,
